 {:seon.task/name "a-merge-is-computed-off-the-writers-step", :seon.task/kind :work, :seon.task/needs #{"seon.merge/a-merge-replays-the-sources-net-change"}, :seon.task/proofs #{}, :seon.task/estimate {seon.writer/branch-diff {:n 1266, :order :linear, :ticks 40000000, :bytes 200000000}}, :seon.task/plan "Owner: data (data, 2026-11-15 18:00, root's order: read the writer stall flow caught). FOUND [M]: on the standing root after the 17:26 pin restart, :writer stepped ONE :in message for 37.15 s (flow's ping, result/c7a3bfc3, recorded 17:32:59), which matches main's 17:32:11-17:32:49 gap with no commit; a sample of that step (/tmp/flow-writer-hang/sample.txt) is 98.5% CPU in konserve's per-key SHA-512 (key->store-key) plus LMDB mdb_get plus Chez GC: a walk reading many stored index nodes. Ruled out at root [M]: collection (last :seon.system/collected 15:37), merge commits (none on any branch in the window but :main/tests' boot merge at 17:26:53), task flights (no :seon.task/execution or outcome change), the union (no caller in src), a branch connect (1.07 ms, result/d1c830f7), merge-base (21-450 ms, result/b03b04c2). CAUSE (the class) [M]: the writer computes merges inside its step: :branch-diff and :merge-candidate (branch-merge, seon.merge/delta-three-way), :merge-main and a resumed :spawn (merge-main-into), :octopus-merge. Each costs O(main's churn since the branch's base), not O(d_branch): :branch-diff's computation on :main/tests-meas6, a branch with ZERO facts of its own, took 15.3 s, 3B ticks, 12.9 GB (result/c6594a16; main moved ~33k txs since its base); the same on :main/tests as it stood at 17:32 (base at its boot merge) took 38 ms (result/d2836a1f). So it is not boot's O(store): it is any merge ask on a stale branch, and while it runs every write in the system waits. Which branch's ask the 37 s step was is [H]: the store records writes, not the message a step held; the ask was read-only (no write followed). PRIOR WORK: flow's approved verdict on landing 6 (seon.merge/a-merge-replays-the-sources-net-change): the merge computes in an seon.fx worker over immutable dbs and the writer only applies, CAS on main's commit; seon.merge/decide (landing 6 slice 1, on jolt/data) judges the branch's facts only. FIX: (1) :branch-diff, :merge-candidate, :merge-main and :spawn's merge compute through one seon.fx effect over references {:branch :commit :t} (docs/storage.md#a-kept-db-value-is-a-reference); the writer applies the answer only if main and the branch are still at those commits, else the asker re-asks; (2) the computation is landing 6's decide (O(d_branch)) for every one of them, deleting branch-merge and delta-three-way's writer callers (landing 10). COST: n = d, the branch's facts since base; O(d) seeks on three dbs plus merge-base's commit walk; ~37k ticks a key [M landing 6]: a 1.3k-fact branch ~40M ticks, a branch with none ~ merge-base alone; lower bound one read of what the branch wrote. The writer's step for any merge then holds only the apply: O(d) checked ops. PROOF to write, red first: a :branch-diff of a branch whose base is far behind main answers while a main write asked after it is answered first (the writer is never held by a merge's read). ALSO (flow's): a step's site names the message's :op, so the next stall names its ask from the ping, not by elimination."}
